2026-10-04T15:49:49.4871892Z   dependency 20 cross-repository pin(s) (3 self-sync, counted separately): driftwood-composed, feeds, fx, ico, insurer, ludlow-composed, nist, platform, tuppence-composed consumed by driftwood, insurer, ludlow, tuppence
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4872937Z     limit      0 of 23 pins name a tag and no commit, so the pin is only as immutable as the tag. Every commit line in this estate is a commented-out placeholder; until one is filled in, 'pinned' means 'pinned to a movable name'.
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4873886Z     limit      3 of 23 pins are a repository syncing itself rather than consuming anybody's policy, and are counted separately: only the 20 cross-repository pins evidence a dependency at all.
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4875253Z     limit      the signature is the sources' own declaration, checked by nothing here: no tag is verified, no Rekor entry is looked up, and estate/verify/provenance/verify-provenance.sh records that this repository's own commits are not keyless-signed either. 'Signed' is therefore a property of the design, not an observation, and the word does no work until something verifies it.
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4877412Z     limit      the consumer repositories are separate by URL and not yet by existence: estate/README.md describes a monorepo-style working tree whose top-level directories become their own GitHub repositories *at split*, and each up.sh rewrites the pinned URL to an in-cluster git server for the offline demo. The pins name real separate repositories; whether those repositories are live is a question this code does not ask.
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4879325Z     limit      read from committed sources, not from a running cluster: this evidences what the repositories declare they consume, never that a control is in force right now. Whether continuous proof of force is required at all is build ticket 65's question and is still open.
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4880801Z     limit      these pins are read from this tool's own repository (.estate-clone), not from any repository 'netflix' owns. The dependency block above evidences what THIS estate consumes; it names no pin, tag or repository belonging to the subject the proposal is about, and 'netflix' is not assumed to have an estate under Flux at all.
2026-10-04T15:49:49.4882406Z   narrowed   Policy-as-code is AN enactment arm, not THE definition of governance. Responses are priced by the FAIR factor they modify and most levers are not code, so if versioned policy were the shape of governance the cross-domain comparison the £ engine exists for could not exist.
